<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lajos Kossuth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1802">1802</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lajos Kossuth was a Hungarian lawyer, journalist and statesman, the leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1848">Hungarian Revolution of 1848</a>. As finance minister and head of the National Defence Committee he organised resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and after the Declaration of Independence of April 1849 he was elected governor-president. After defeat by Austrian and Russian armies he went into exile, where he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth.jpg" alt="Lajos Kossuth" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Southworth &amp; Hawes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Lajos_Kossuth,_1852.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lajos Kossuth was born on 19 September 1802 at Monok, in Zemplén county, the eldest of five children in a Lutheran family of the lower nobility with Slovak origins. His father, László, held a small estate and worked as a lawyer. The family moved to Olaszliszka in 1803 and to Sátoraljaújhely in 1808. Kossuth entered national politics as deputy to Count Hunyady at the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, which met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>. His marriage to Meszlényi, a Catholic, took place on the day of his release from prison, and neither would convert. The mixed marriage caused a scandal and shaped his later defence of such unions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In January 1841 Kossuth became editor of the Pesti Hírlap. Authorities expected censorship to blunt him, but his editorials on the economy, social injustice and the legal inequality of the common people combined into a coherent reform programme, and the paper reached the then immense circulation of 7,000 copies. He differed with Count Széchenyi, who wanted reform slowly and carefully to avoid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> intervention and favoured a limited suffrage, while Kossuth saw rights as unitary and pointed to universal suffrage.</p><p>On 3 March 1848, after news of the revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, he demanded parliamentary government for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. On 7 December 1848 the Diet refused to acknowledge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> as king and called the nation to arms. From then until the revolution collapsed Kossuth, as elected regent-president, was Hungary's ruler. After defeat he crossed into Ottoman territory, where the authorities refused to surrender him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Feted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1851, he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 6 December 1851 and gave over 600 speeches in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> for eight years, working with exiles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1861 Kossuth moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. On the day before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867</a> he published the open letter called the Cassandra letter, which condemned the settlement and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferenc-deak">Ferenc Deák</a> and predicted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> would fall with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. He remained committed to full independence and never returned to Hungary. In 1890 he lost his Hungarian citizenship under the law of 1879, having said he would rather be a man without a country than swear an oath to the Habsburgs.</p><p>Kossuth died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> on 20 March 1894. His body was taken to Pest, where Mór Jókai delivered the funeral oration. The Party of Independence and '48, formed in 1884, regarded him as its spiritual leader, and his son Ferenc Kossuth became Minister for Trade in 1906. The square before the Hungarian Parliament Building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lajos Kossuth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Habsburg lands Metternich resigned and went into exile, Ferdinand I abdicated in December 1848 in favour of Franz Joseph I, and <b>Lajos Kossuth</b> led the Hungarian government.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · People</a></li><li><blockquote>After the spring campaign drove back Austrian forces, the Hungarian parliament, meeting in Debrecen, adopted the Declaration of Independence on 14 April 1849, deposing the Habsburg dynasty and electing <b>Lajos Kossuth</b> governor-president.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-state-1849">Hungarian State (1849)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Monok, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,21.15&amp;year=1861" aria-label="Monok on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5042 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lajos Kossuth"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5492 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5492 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,21.15&amp;year=1861">What was here in 1861? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let me, before I go to work, have some hours of rest upon this soil of freedom, your happy home. Freedom and home; what heavenly music in those two words! Alas! I have no home, and the freedom of my people is downtrodden.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech on his welcome to New York, December 1851 &#183; original &#183; spoken in English, in exile after the Hungarian revolution &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/On_His_Welcome_to_New_York" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My beloved native land! thy very sufferings make thee but dearer to my heart; thy bleeding image dwells with me when I wake, as it rests with me in the short moments of my restless sleep.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech on his welcome to New York, December 1851 &#183; original &#183; spoken in English, in exile after the Hungarian revolution &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/On_His_Welcome_to_New_York" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have no idea but thee; I have no feeling but thee.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech on his welcome to New York, December 1851 &#183; original &#183; spoken in English, of his homeland &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/On_His_Welcome_to_New_York" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…where the great, and I hope, the last battle will be fought between liberty and despotism…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech on his welcome to New York, December 1851 &#183; original &#183; spoken in English, in exile after the Hungarian revolution &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/On_His_Welcome_to_New_York" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;That spirit is a power for deeds, but is yet no deed in itself. Despotism and oppression never yet were beaten except by heroic resistance. That is a sad necessity,—but it is a necessity nevertheless.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at New York, December 1851 (Select Speeches of Kossuth, 1854) &#183; original &#183; spoken in English, in exile after the Hungarian revolution &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10691/pg10691-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;So long as despots exist, and have the means to pay, they will find men to calumniate those who are opposed to tyranny.&#8221;</p><footer>Select Speeches of Kossuth (1854), in America, 1851-52 &#183; original &#183; spoken in English, in exile after the Hungarian revolution &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10691/pg10691-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
